            "wrap_link_url": "",
            "enable_text_shadow": false,
            "title": "<h2>Wait, I need to do math?</h2>",
            "description": "<p>The other issue with tubing is the size. And this… gets confusing. First of all, click on this chart to help get a better understanding of the idea.</p><p>The important thing is the difference between ID and OD. Sometimes tubing is measured by the OD, or Outer Diameter. This is most common with tubing that is used in fabrication, or whenever we’re interested in how thick the tubing is to the observer. Other times, tubing or pipe is measured by the ID, or Inner Diameter. ID is important when we’re interested in how much stuff can fit inside the tube, usually water or air. In the hoop world, when someone tells you “Oh I only use <a href=\"https://moodhoops.com/polypro-hoops/\">3/4″ polypro</a>” they probably mean 3/4″ OD tubing (about the size of a penny), but sometimes they are actually referring to <a href=\"https://moodhoops.com/shop-category/hoops/beginner-hoops/\">3/4″ ID tubing</a> which is about 1″ OD (about the size of a quarter). Confused yet?</p><p>For more information on hoop sizing, check out our <a href=\"https://moodhoops.com/sizing-guide/\">Hoop Sizing Guide</a>.</p>",
